    "c4": {
      "anchor": "p-bubbly-in-monaco#field-1",
      "kind": "field",
      "label": "Bubbly in Monaco",
      "excerpt": "Spell's own code editor, Monaco, shows Spell as coloured text, not in the bubbly",
      "text": "Again, keep as much of the bubbly CSS names/structures as you can, and in bubbly.css file.  repo-split's monaco implementation was super hard to edit.\n\nAlso, I'm going to have more notes about \"this rule should present this way\" -- plan for that.",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-11T06:36:04.871Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "quote": "Spell's own code editor",
      "offset": 0,
      "edited": "2026-11-11T06:37:15.746Z",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-11T06:38:58.075Z",
          "html": "<p>Yes: one stylesheet, <code>bubbly.css</code>, will style both the page drawing and Monaco, using the same <code>bubbly-*</code> class names and <code>--bubbly-*</code> variables, with Monaco-only rules in one marked section. VS Code cannot load CSS, so it will read the same values from that file. I also planned one table, by rule name, saying how each rule presents, so your &ldquo;this rule should present this way&rdquo; notes are one-line edits (built in P49, read by P50 and P51, your notes land in P52).</p>"
        }
      ],
      "claim": {
        "by": "owenoak",
        "agent": "claude",
        "at": "2026-11-11T06:38:16.031Z"
      }
    }
  },
